+# Life Data Analysis Workflow (best-practice synthesis)
+
+Paraphrased synthesis for planning and Q&A grounding. Consult the linked
+primary sources for authoritative detail.
+
+## 1. Classify the data first
+
+Life data falls into four types (NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook, Chapter 8,
+*Assessing Product Reliability*):
+
+- **Complete data**: every unit tested to failure; exact failure times known.
+- **Right-censored (suspensions)**: some units removed or still running at the
+  end of observation; only a lower bound on their life is known.
+- **Interval-censored**: failure known to fall between two inspection times.
+- **Left-censored**: failure known only to precede some observed time.
+
+Record units, failure definition, operating environment, and censoring
+semantics before any fitting. Ambiguous censoring is a data defect, not a
+modeling choice.
+
+Sources:
+
+- NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods, Chapter 8 (public domain) —
+  <https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/>
+- MIL-HDBK-338B §8, Reliability Data Collection and Analysis (free PDF)
+
+## 2. Choose the estimation method by rule
+
+- **Rank regression on Y (RRY)**: least-squares fit on probability paper using
+  median ranks (Bernard/Herd-Johnson). Preferred default for complete and
+  small-sample data; the correlation coefficient doubles as a fit diagnostic.
+- **Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)**: preferred for heavily censored data
+  with few exact failures and for interval data, where rank regression
+  degrades.
+
+Sources:
+
+- NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook, Chapter 8 (public domain)
+- IEC 61649 *Weibull analysis* (IEC TC 56; licensed standard — scope cited,
+  not reproduced)
+
+## 3. Justify the distribution
+
+Candidate life distributions include Weibull, lognormal, exponential, and
+normal. Whatever is assumed must be stated with its goodness-of-fit evidence
+(Kolmogorov–Smirnov, correlation-coefficient, and likelihood-value tests are
+the standard comparison set); a Weibull-only tool must say so.
+
+Standardized Weibull procedures and confidence-interval methods are specified
+in IEC 61649 *Weibull analysis* (IEC TC 56; licensed standard — scope cited,
+not reproduced). Public cross-check of Weibull definitions:
+<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weibull_distribution>.
+
+## 4. Report with uncertainty and sampling risk
+
+- Always attach confidence bounds to fitted lines and to derived lives
+  (B10/B20/MTBF). IEC 61649 treats intervals as part of the analysis.
+- State sample size vs suspensions; small samples widen bounds and weaken
+  warranty claims. Formal demonstration risk follows sampling plans such as
+  MIL-STD-690 *Failure Rate Sampling Plans and Procedures*.
+- One-page reports must trace back to tool outputs: method, input summary,
+  units, assumptions, warnings, and cited sources — never recomputed prose.
+
+## 5. Tool mapping in this repository
+
+| Workflow step | MCP tool |
+|---|---|
+| Empirical Kaplan-Meier reference (non-parametric) | `analyze_nonparametric_life_data` |
+| Fit one life-distribution family | `fit_life_distribution` |
+| Rank/select one or many families (AIC + KM cue) | `select_life_distribution` |
+| Fit 2-parameter Weibull (RRY/MLE, bootstrap bounds, plot) | `analyze_weibull` |
+| B-life percentiles (B10/B20/B30) | `calculate_b_life` |
+| MTBF from operating history | `calculate_mtbf` |
+| Procedure/handbook lookup with citations | `search_reliability_knowledge` |
+| One-page markdown report from tool outputs | `export_reliability_report` |
+| Structured plan (data check, KM, select, fit, report) | `create_life_data_plan` |
+| Grounded Q&A evidence pack | `ask_life_data_question` |
