+# Weibull Analysis Skill
+
+## Overview
+
+Performs 2-parameter Weibull distribution fitting to failure time data with optional right-censored suspensions. Generates probability paper plots with Bernard/Herd-Johnson median ranks and estimates shape (β) and scale (η) parameters using Rank Regression on Y (RRY) or Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE).
+
+## Purpose
+
+Weibull analysis is the cornerstone of reliability engineering for:
+
+- **Life data analysis**: Characterizing failure distributions from test or field data
+- **Warranty analysis**: Estimating B-life (time at which X% of units fail)
+- **Design reviews**: Providing defensible plots and parameters for design verification
+- **Reliability prediction**: Estimating failure rates and MTBF
+
+The skill ensures numeric accuracy by performing all calculations in Python code rather than allowing the LLM to invent values.
+
+## MCP Tool
+
+**Tool Name**: `analyze_weibull`
+
+**Signature**:
+```python
+def analyze_weibull(
+    failure_times: list[float],
+    title: str = "Weibull Probability Plot",
+    censored_times: list[float] | None = None,
+    method: str = "rry",
+    confidence: float = 0.9,
+) -> dict[str, Any]
+```
+
+**Parameters**:
+- `failure_times`: List of failure times (must be > 0, minimum 2 values)
+- `title`: Plot title (default: "Weibull Probability Plot")
+- `censored_times`: Optional list of right-censored suspension times (≥ 0)
+- `method`: Fitting method - "rry" (default) or "mle"
+- `confidence`: Two-sided bootstrap confidence level (0.5 to < 1.0, default 0.9)
+
+**Returns**:
+```json
+{
+  "beta": float,              // Shape parameter
+  "eta": float,               // Scale parameter (characteristic life)
+  "mtbf": float,              // Mean time between failures
+  "b10": float,               // B10 life (10% failure)
+  "b20": float,               // B20 life (20% failure)
+  "b30": float,               // B30 life (30% failure)
+  "method": str,              // Fitting method used
+  "sample_size": int,         // Total sample (failures + suspensions)
+  "failures": int,            // Number of failures
+  "suspensions": int,         // Number of suspensions
+  "file": str,                // Plot filename (e.g., "weibull_abc123.png")
+  "message": str              // Human-readable summary
+}
+```
+
+**Plot Output**:
+- PNG plot saved to `backend/static/`
+- Filename returned in `file` field
+- Probability paper with:
+  - Failure data points with median ranks
+  - Suspension data points (right-censored)
+  - Fitted Weibull line
+  - Bootstrap confidence bounds (if confidence > 0.5)
+  - β, η, and sample size in legend
+
+## Implementation
+
+### Tool Registration
+**File**: `backend/src/domains/reliability/server.py`
+
+```python
+@mcp.tool()
+def analyze_weibull(
+    failure_times: list[float],
+    title: str = "Weibull Probability Plot",
+    censored_times: list[float] | None = None,
+    method: str = "rry",
+    confidence: float = 0.9,
+) -> CallToolResult:
+    """Fit 2-parameter Weibull data, plot probability paper, and return statistics."""
+    result = _weibull.analyze_weibull(...)
+    # Returns structured content + MCP image block
+```
+
+### Tool Implementation
+**File**: `backend/src/domains/reliability/tools/weibull.py`
+
+```python
+def analyze_weibull(
+    failure_times: list[float],
+    title: str = "Weibull Probability Plot",
+    censored_times: list[float] | None = None,
+    method: str = "rry",
+    confidence: float = 0.9,
+) -> dict[str, Any]:
+    # Validation
+    if len(failure_times) < 2:
+        raise ValueError("at least two failure times are required")
+    if any(value <= 0 for value in failure_times):
+        raise ValueError("failure times must be greater than zero")
+
+    # Compute with plot generation
+    with PLOT_LOCK:
+        result = plot_weibull_analysis(
+            np.asarray(failure_times, dtype=float),
+            title,
+            censored_times=censored_times,
+            method=method,
+            confidence=confidence,
+        )
+
+    # Normalize for JSON serialization
+    normalized = {key: json_safe(value) for key, value in result.items()}
+    return normalized
+```
+
+### Core Mathematics
+**File**: `backend/src/domains/reliability/weibull_fit.py`
+
+Key functions:
+- `median_rank_benard(n, i)`: Bernard's approximation for median ranks
+- `fit_weibull_rry()`: Rank Regression on Y fitting
+- `fit_weibull_mle()`: Maximum Likelihood Estimation
+- `b_life(beta, eta, percentage)`: B-life calculation
+- `bootstrap_bounds()`: Bootstrap confidence intervals
+
+### Plot Generation
+**File**: `backend/src/domains/reliability/life_data_analysis_weibull.py`
+
+Key function:
+- `plot_weibull_analysis()`: Generates matplotlib probability paper plot
+
+Plot features:
+- Log-log axes (Weibull probability paper)
+- Failure points with median ranks
+- Suspension points (right-censored)
+- Fitted line
+- Bootstrap bounds (if requested)
+- Professional styling for design reviews
+
+## Mathematical Background
+
+### Weibull Distribution
+
+Cumulative distribution function:
+```
+F(t) = 1 - exp(-(t/η)^β)
+```
+
+Where:
+- β (beta): Shape parameter (failure rate behavior)
+  - β < 1: Decreasing failure rate (infant mortality)
+  - β = 1: Constant failure rate (random failures)
+  - β > 1: Increasing failure rate (wear-out)
+- η (eta): Scale parameter (characteristic life, 63.2% failure point)
+
+### Median Rank Calculation
+
+Bernard's approximation (used for right-censored data):
+```
+MR = (i - 0.3) / (n + 0.4)
+```
+
+Where:
+- i: Failure order number
+- n: Total sample size (failures + suspensions)
+
+### Rank Regression on Y (RRY)
+
+Linear regression on transformed Weibull equation:
+```
+ln(ln(1/(1-F))) = β * ln(t) - β * ln(η)
+```
+
+Plot ln(t) on x-axis, ln(ln(1/(1-F))) on y-axis, fit line through failure points.
+
+### Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE)
+
+Maximizes likelihood function:
+```
+L(β, η) = ∏[f(t_i)] * ∏[R(s_j)]
+```
+
+Where:
+- f(t_i): PDF for failures
+- R(s_j): Reliability function for suspensions
+
+Requires numerical optimization (scipy.optimize).
+
+### B-Life Calculation
+
+Time at which percentage P of units have failed:
+```
+B_P = η * (-ln(1 - P/100))^(1/β)
+```
+
+Common values:
+- B10: 10% failure
+- B20: 20% failure
+- B30: 30% failure
+
+### MTBF Calculation
+
+Mean time between failures for Weibull:
+```
+MTBF = η * Γ(1 + 1/β)
+```
+
+Where Γ is the gamma function.
+
